-- title:
--   Even part maps to a type-preserving tree lattice in PGL₂
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$ and a discrete valuation ring $\mathcal O$ (a commutative domain) with an irreducible element $\pi$ whose residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements, together with a fraction field $K_0$ of $\mathcal O$. Let $v\det : GL_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) be a homomorphism characterised by the property that $v\det(g) = n$ exactly when $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$. Let $G$ be a group with homomorphisms $\sigma : G \to GL_2(K_0)$ and $\rho : G \to PGL(2,K_0)$ such that $\rho(g)$ is the class of $\sigma(g)$ for all $g$, let $\Gamma \le G$, and let $\Gamma' \le G$ be a subgroup whose elements are exactly the $x \in \Gamma$ with $v\det(\sigma x)$ even. Write $H = \rho(\Gamma')$, acting on the vertex set of the Bruhat–Tits tree, i.e. on homothety classes of full $\mathcal O$-lattices in $K_0^2$, with adjacency generated by the adjacency relation on lattices. Assume that for every vertex $v$ the set of elements of $H$ fixing $v$ is finite, and that there is a finite set $S$ of vertices such that every vertex is carried into $S$ by some element of $H$. The conclusion is a sevenfold conjunction: every vertex stabiliser in $H$ is finite; the set of $H$-orbits of vertices is finite; the set of $H$-orbits of darts of the tree is finite; $H$ is contained in the subgroup of $PGL(2,K_0)$ of elements $g$ satisfying $d(v_0, g\cdot w) \equiv d(v_0, w) \pmod 2$ for all vertices $w$, where $v_0$ is the class of the standard lattice; no element of $H$ sends a dart to its reversal; $H$ is finitely generated; and the relative index of $H$ in $\rho(\Gamma)$ is nonzero. The last clause asserts only nonvanishing of the relative index, not the classical bound by $2$.
--
--   This is the verification that the image in $PGL_2(K_0)$ of the even part of a discrete, cocompact subgroup satisfies the axioms of a type-preserving, inversion-free, finitely generated tree lattice of finite index in the image of the whole group, in the form required for Mumford's uniformisation and the Čerednik–Drinfeld description of quotients. It is used by the statements producing formal quotient data, Schottky subgroups of even type, and the geometry of the Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_BruhatTits_treeLattice_facts_map_evenPart.lean

